{#
	The tenant, the modules they have, and the way out.

	Tabs rather than plain links: with more than two modules the row needs to say
	which one you are in, and an underline does that more quietly than a filled
	pill. The icon comes from the module's own definition, so a module chooses how
	it appears the same way it chooses its label.

	The bar keeps its own background; the active tab takes the *page* colour, so it
	reads as opening onto what is below rather than dissolving into the bar. That
	needs an override — see .navbar .nav-tabs in app.css.
#}
<nav class="navbar navbar-expand bg-body border-bottom mb-4 pb-0">
	<div class="container page-shell flex-wrap">
		<div class="d-flex w-100 align-items-center">
			{# Their company name once they have set one, the registry's label for them
			   until then — see AppChrome::getName (XIV-12). #}
			{# A logo when the deployment supplied one (XIV-48), the name in text
			   otherwise — which is what a clean checkout does. The name stays as
			   the alt text either way, so the mark is never the only thing
			   saying whose installation this is. #}
			<a class="navbar-brand fw-semibold d-flex align-items-center gap-2" href="{{ path('dashboard') }}">
				{% if chrome.logo %}
					<img src="{{ asset(chrome.logo) }}" alt="" height="28" class="d-block">
				{% endif %}
				{{ chrome.name }}
			</a>

			{#
				One menu under the signed-in person's name, where there used to be a
				row of buttons (XIV-77).

				**The comment this replaces was right, and it was right about three.**
				It read: "Plain links rather than a dropdown under the email.
				Bootstrap's JavaScript is shipped now (for tooltips, XIV-8), so a
				dropdown would open — but three links that are always visible beat a
				menu that has to be found first." That is a true sentence about
				profile, users and account. It was never an argument that dropdowns
				are wrong; it was an argument about how much there was to show, and
				the amount changed. The store (XIV-6) made four and sign out was
				always the fifth, at which point "always visible" stops being a
				feature: five icons in a row is a row of icons, and nobody reads a row
				of icons — they hunt through it every time. A menu has to be found
				once. So this is not overturning that decision; it is the same decision
				with its premise moved, which is why it is quoted here rather than
				deleted. Do not re-litigate it back without a count to go with it.

				**Sign out went inside**, for the same reason and against the same
				objection. Keeping it out was worth a second click on a shared
				machine, except that signing out is the rarest thing anybody does here
				— most people never do — and optimising a layout for its least-used
				item is exactly how it ended up looking like this. Every desktop
				operating system puts it two clicks deep. It is still a POST form with
				a submit button inside the menu, not a link: moving a control into a
				menu must not quietly turn a state change into something a prefetcher
				can trigger.

				**Nothing here is a security control.** Each item is hidden from
				somebody who may not use it as a courtesy — a menu full of doors that
				answer "no" is a worse way to learn your job than a short menu — and
				every route refuses on its own account regardless.
			#}
			{#
				Which route is being served, so the menu below can say where you
				are. The module tabs cannot answer this: user management and the
				account page are not modules, and marking Overview active on them
				said "you are on the dashboard" while plainly not being there.
			#}
			{% set route = app.request.attributes.get('_route')|default('') %}
			{% set on_users = route starts with 'user_' or route starts with 'group_' %}
			{% set on_profile = route starts with 'tenant_profile' %}
			{% set on_store = route starts with 'store_' %}

			{#
				The menu's items, built once and read twice: the list draws them, and
				the closed button wears whichever of them you are currently on. One
				list rather than two passes of the same conditions, so the shut menu
				can never advertise a page this person is not allowed to open.

				Your own account first, then the three things about the installation,
				because the menu hangs off your own name and that is the item it is
				named after.
			#}
			{% set items = [{
				route: 'account',
				icon: 'person-circle',
				label: 'nav.account'|trans,
				current: route == 'account',
			}] %}

			{#
				Granted rather than admin-only (XIV-12): the instance's settings are
				somebody's job, and not always the same somebody who manages users.
			#}
			{% if can('view', chrome.profileArea) %}
				{% set items = items|merge([{
					route: 'tenant_profile',
					icon: 'building-gear',
					label: 'nav.profile'|trans,
					current: on_profile,
				}]) %}
			{% endif %}

			{#
				The store (XIV-6), granted on its own axis rather than on any
				module's: browsing is about no module and installing is about one this
				customer has not got, so there is nothing for a module grant to attach
				to (§8.4.3).
			#}
			{% if can('browse', chrome.store) %}
				{% set items = items|merge([{
					route: 'store_index',
					icon: 'shop',
					label: 'nav.store'|trans,
					current: on_store,
				}]) %}
			{% endif %}

			{% if is_granted('ROLE_ADMIN') %}
				{% set items = items|merge([{
					route: 'user_index',
					icon: 'people',
					label: 'nav.users'|trans,
					current: on_users,
				}]) %}
			{% endif %}

			{#
				**How a shut menu still says where you are.** A dropdown that only
				marks its current item once it is open would take the answer away from
				the bar entirely: you would have to open a menu to find out which page
				you are reading. So the item you are on climbs onto the button — its
				own icon and label, after the name, with the button drawn as active.
				It is the same information the old row of buttons carried, in the one
				place still visible, and it costs nothing on the pages that are not in
				this menu because there is nothing to show there.

				The alternative considered was marking the button active without
				saying which item — one bit instead of the answer, which reads as "you
				are somewhere in here" and sends you into the menu anyway. Not worth
				the pixels it saves.
			#}
			{% set here = items|filter(item => item.current)|first %}
			{% set me = avatar(app.user) %}

			<div class="dropdown ms-auto">
				<button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary dropdown-toggle d-flex align-items-center gap-2{{ here ? ' active' : '' }}"
				        type="button" data-bs-toggle="dropdown" aria-expanded="false">
					{#
						The avatar. Initials in a circle, coloured from the email —
						generated in {@see App\Twig\Avatar} and never fetched, so no
						request leaves the browser for it and no third party is told
						who is signed in. Where an uploaded picture would go later is
						written down there, on the class that would grow the field.

						Decorative, hence aria-hidden: it is the name beside it drawn
						a second way, and a screen reader announcing "N B, Nina
						Baumgartner" is reading the same thing twice.
					#}
					<span class="avatar" style="--avatar-hue: {{ me.hue }}" aria-hidden="true">{{ me.initials }}</span>
					<span class="topbar-name text-truncate">{{ app.user.name }}</span>
					{% if here %}
						{#
							Below the small breakpoint the label goes and the icon
							stays, so the button cannot grow into the brand on a phone.
							The label is then repeated to the accessible tree, because
							`d-none` is `display: none` and takes a thing away from a
							screen reader as thoroughly as from the eye — and "which
							page am I on" answered only in an icon is answered only for
							people who can see icons.
						#}
						<span class="vr d-none d-sm-inline mx-1 opacity-50"></span>
						<i class="bi bi-{{ here.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
						<span class="d-none d-sm-inline">{{ here.label }}</span>
						<span class="visually-hidden d-sm-none">{{ here.label }}</span>
					{% endif %}
				</button>

				{#
					Aligned to the button's right edge, which is also the page's: a
					menu that opened leftwards from here would hang off the screen on
					a narrow one. Bootstrap keeps it on screen from there by itself.
				#}
				<ul class="dropdown-menu dropdown-menu-end">
					{% for item in items %}
						<li>
							{#
								`aria-current` written inside an if rather than as a
								printed expression: Twig escapes the quotes in
								`{{ '...="page"' }}`, which the browser then reads back
								as a value with the quotes in it. It survived because an
								invalid value still counts as "current" — but only by
								accident, and it is not what this is meant to say.
							#}
							<a class="dropdown-item d-flex align-items-center gap-2{{ item.current ? ' active' : '' }}"
							   href="{{ path(item.route) }}"{% if item.current %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
								<i class="bi bi-{{ item.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
								<span>{{ item.label }}</span>
							</a>
						</li>
					{% endfor %}

					<li><hr class="dropdown-divider"></li>

					<li>
						<form method="post" action="{{ path('logout') }}">
							<button type="submit" class="dropdown-item d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
								<i class="bi bi-box-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i>
								<span>{{ 'nav.sign_out'|trans }}</span>
							</button>
						</form>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>

		<ul class="nav nav-tabs border-bottom-0 w-100 mt-2">
			{% set current = app.request.attributes.get('module') %}
			<li class="nav-item">
				{# The dashboard route itself, not merely "no module": /users has no
				   module either and is plainly not the dashboard. #}
				<a class="nav-link{{ route == 'dashboard' ? ' active' : '' }}" href="{{ path('dashboard') }}">
					<i class="bi bi-grid" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					<span class="ms-1">{{ 'nav.overview'|trans }}</span>
				</a>
			</li>
			{% for module in chrome.modules %}
				<li class="nav-item">
					<a class="nav-link{{ current == module.key ? ' active' : '' }}"
					   href="{{ path('module_index', {module: module.key}) }}">
						<i class="bi bi-{{ module.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
						<span class="ms-1">{{ module.label }}</span>
					</a>
				</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ul>
	</div>
</nav>
